'''Caucasian Albanian''' (also called '''Old Udi''', '''Aluan''' or '''Aghwan''')<ref name=GS201>{{cite journal|last1=Gippert|first1=Jost|last2=Schulze|first2=Wolfgang |title=Some remarks on the Caucasian Albanian palimpsests|journal=Iran and the Caucasus|year=2007 |volume=11 |issue=2|doi=10.1163/157338407X265441|pages=201–211}}</ref> is an [[extinct language|extinct]] member of the [[Northeast Caucasian languages]]. It was spoken in [[Caucasian Albania]], which stretched from current day south [[Dagestan]] to [[Azerbaijan]]. Linguists believe it is an early linguistic predecessor to the endangered Northeast Caucasian [[Udi language]].<ref>Zaza Aleksidze, [http://azer.com/aiweb/categories/magazine/ai113_folder/113_articles/113_zaza_udi_language.html "Udi Language: Comparing Ancient Albanian with Contemporary Udi]," in Azerbaijan International, Vol. 11:3 (Autumn 2003), p. 43.</ref> The distinct [[Caucasian Albanian alphabet]] used 52 letters.

Caucasian Albanian possibly corresponds to the "Gargarian" language identified by medieval Armenian historians. Despite its name, Caucasian Albanian bears no linguistic relationship whatsoever with the [[Albanian language]] spoken in [[Albania]], which belongs to the [[Indo-European languages|Indo-European family]].

==Discovery and decipherment==
The existence of the Caucasian Albanian literature was known only indirectly before the late 20th century. [[Koryun]]'s ''Life of Mashtots'', written in the 5th century but only surviving in much later corrupted manuscripts, and [[Movses Kaghankatvatsi]]'s ''History of the Caucasian Albanians'', written in the 10th century, attribute the conversion of the Caucasian Albanians to Christianity to two missionaries, Enoch and Dana, and the creation of the Caucasian Albanian alphabet to the Armenian scholar [[Mesrop Mashtots]]. A certain Bishop Jeremiah then translated the [[Christian Bible]] into their language. As recently as 1977, [[Bruce Metzger]] could write that "nothing of [this] version has survived".<ref>[[Bruce M. Metzger]], ''The Early Versions of the New Testament: Their Origin, Transmission, and Limitations'' (Oxford University Press, 1977), p. 303.</ref>

In 1996, [[Zaza Aleksidze]] of the Centre of Manuscripts in [[Tbilisi]], [[Georgia (country)|Georgia]], discovered a [[palimpsest]]<ref>Zaza Aleksidze, "Caucasian Albanian Script: The Significance of Decipherment," [http://azer.com/aiweb/categories/magazine/ai113_folder/113_articles/113_zaza_significance.html Azerbaijan International, Vol. 11:3 (Autumn 2003]), p. 56.</ref> at [[Saint Catherine's Monastery]] on [[Mount Sinai]], [[Egypt]], with an unknown script.<ref>Zaza Aleksidze and Betty Blair, in "Caucasian Albanian Alphabet, Ancient Script Discovered in the Ashes," in [http://azer.com/aiweb/categories/magazine/ai113_folder/113_articles/113_zaza_script_ashes.html Azerbaijan International, Vol. 11:3 (Autumn 2003), pp. 38-41].</ref> He went on to identify the alphabet as Caucasian Albanian,<ref>Zaza Aleksidze and Betty Blair, [http://azer.com/aiweb/categories/magazine/ai113_folder/113_articles/113_zaza_quick_facts.html "Quick Facts: The Caucasian Albanian Script,"] Azerbaijan International, Vol. 11:3 (Autumn 2003), p. 43.</ref> and to identify the manuscript as an early Christian [[lectionary]] from about the 5th or 6th century. The lectionary may be the earliest extant lectionary in the Christian religion.<ref>Zaza Aleksidze and Betty Blair, in "The Albanian Script: The Process - How Its Secrets Were Revealed," in [http://azer.com/aiweb/categories/magazine/ai113_folder/113_articles/113_zaza_secrets_revealed.html Azerbaijan International, Vol. 11:3 (Autumn 2003), pp. 41-51.].</ref>

Then linguists [[Jost Gippert]] and Wolfgang Schulze got involved with the [[Caucasian Albanian alphabet]].<ref name ="Schulze">{{cite web|url=http://udilang.narod.ru/papers/Schulze_History-of-Udi.pdf|title=Towards a history of Udi|publisher =Papers of the IFEA Round Table|author=Wolfgang Schulze|accessdate=2011-01-18}}</ref> Specialized x-ray equipment was used, which made it possible to read the Caucasian Albanian palimpsest texts in their entirety.<ref name="Alexidze">{{cite web|url=http://www.science.org.ge/2007-vol1/161-166.pdf |title=Discovery and Decipherment of Caucasian Albanian Writing |publisher=Bulletin of the Georgian National Academy of Sciences |author=Zaza Alexidze |year=2007 |accessdate=2011-01-18 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20110721030511/http://www.science.org.ge/2007-vol1/161-166.pdf |archivedate=July 21, 2011 }}</ref> A list of Caucasian Albanian month names, which survived in a number of medieval [[manuscript]]s, gave one of the clues to the language.<ref name ="Schulze"/> In 2017, two additional texts of Caucasian Albanian were discovered in Saint Catherine's Monastery.<ref>[https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/middle-east/lost-languages-found-ancient-manuscript-parchment-saint-catherine-monastery-sinai-peninsula-egypt-a7916346.html Scientists find languages not used since Dark Ages among ancient manuscripts recovered from monastery].</ref> The original text on the palimpsests was erased anywhere between the 4th and 12th century.<ref>Sarah Lasgow [http://www.atlasobscura.com/articles/saint-catherines-monastery-palimpsests-lost-language-undertext "Found: Hidden Examples of Long-Lost Languages in Centuries-Old Palimpsests"]</ref>

== History ==
The Greek author [[Strabo]] (died 1st century AD) reports that the Caucasian Albanians spoke 26 different languages "for want of mutual intercourse with each other". It is supposed that most of the Caucasian Albanians were speakers of [[Lezgic languages]], along with some [[Iranian languages|Iranian]] and [[Kartvelian languages|Kartvelian]]-speaking tribes. The Caucasian Albanian language probably developed from the language (or dialect) of one of the Lezgic-speaking tribes of the country which had risen to socio-economic and cultural prominence; Aleksan Hakobyan refers to it as a "pan-Albanian ''[[Koiné language|Koine]]''". After the creation of the [[Caucasian Albanian alphabet]] in the early 5th century, the Caucasian Albanian language became the official language of the [[Church of Caucasian Albania|Caucasian Albanian Church]]. However, Caucasian Albanian does not appear to have found broad acceptance as the common language of the Albanian tribes, and from the beginning it shared its official status in the Church with [[Armenian language|Armenian]]. It lost its official status within the Church after the shift of the Albanian capital to [[Partaw]], south of the [[Kura (South Caucasus river)|Kura River]]. It survived as the language of church services in the Caucasian Albanian lands north of the Kura but ultimately fell into disuse as a written language, giving way to Armenian and [[Georgian language|Georgian]].<ref name="Hakobyan476">{{cite book |last=Hakobyan |first=Aleksan |url=https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9783110794687-014/html |title=Caucasian Albania: An International Handbook |date=2023 |publisher=De Gruyter |isbn=978-3-11-079468-7 |editor-last=Gippert |editor-first=Jost |pages=476–477, 480–481, 483 |chapter=12. The Ethnic Situation in Antique and Medieval Caucasian Albania |doi=10.1515/9783110794687-014 |editor2-last=Dum-Tragut |editor2-first=Jasmine|editor-link=Jost Gippert}}</ref>

The geographer [[Istakhri]] (writing {{Circa|930}}) reports that ''al-rānīya'' was spoken in "the country of Bardha'a [the Arabic name for Partaw]". (''[[Arran (Caucasus)|Arran]]'' or ''al-Ran'' is the term used in Muslim sources for Caucasian Albania.) His continuators, [[Ibn Hawqal]] and [[al-Maqdisi]], give slightly differing information: Ibn Hawqal writes that the "inhabitants of Bardha'a speak ''al-rānīya''" while al-Maqdisi says "in Arran [they speak] ''al-rānīya''". A number of scholars conclude from these reports that Caucasian Albanian was spoken in Bardha'a and the surrounding area in the 10th century. Hakobyan thinks that the speakers of ''al-rānīya''/Caucasian Albanian were only located to the north of the Kura.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Akopian |first=Aleksan |title=Albaniia-Aluank v greko-latinskikh i drevnearmianskikh istochnikakh |publisher=Gitutyun |year=2022 |isbn=978-5-8080-1485-5 |edition=2nd, rev. |location=Yerevan |pages=126, note 3 |language=ru |script-title=ru:Албания-Алуанк в греко-латинских и древнеармянских источниках |trans-title=Albania-Aluank in the Greek-Latin and Old-Armenian Sources |orig-date=1st pub. 1987}}</ref> According to Wolfgang Schulze, "it is a matter of dispute whether this language [''al-rānīya''] was 'Albanian' at all. Instead, one might assume that we have to deal with a local Northwest Iranian variety."<ref>{{cite book |last=Schulze |first=Wolfgang |url=https://www.degruyter.com/document/doi/10.1515/9783110601268-008/html |title=Sprachen, Völker und Phantome: Sprach- und kulturwissenschaftliche Studien zur Ethnizität |date=22 October 2018 |publisher=De Gruyter |isbn=978-3-11-060126-8 |editor-last=Mumm |editor-first=Peter-Arnold |pages=290–291 |chapter=Caucasian Albanian and the Question of Language and Ethnicity |doi=10.1515/9783110601268-008}}</ref>

The [[Udi language]], spoken by the [[Udi people]] in Armenia, Georgia, Azerbaijan and elsewhere, is closely related to Caucasian Albanian and may be its direct descendant.<ref>{{cite book |last=Schulze |first=Wolfgang |chapter=5. Caucasian Albanian and Modern Udi |date=5 June 2023 |title=Caucasian Albania: An International Handbook |pages=231, 233 |editor-last=Gippert |editor-first=Jost |author-link2=Jost Gippert |url=https://www.degruyterbrill.com/document/doi/10.1515/9783110794687-005/html |publisher=De Gruyter |doi=10.1515/9783110794687-005 |isbn=978-3-11-079468-7 |last2=Gippert |first2=Jost |editor2-last=Dum-Tragut |editor2-first=Jasmine|location=Berlin|quote=[…] it can safely be stated today that the language of the present-day Udis is a close relative, if not a direct descendant, of the Caucasian Albanian language of the Middle Ages, with an interrelation which is similar to the one between Old Armenian (Grabar) and Modern East Armenian.}}</ref>

==Texts==
The deciphered text of the lectionary includes excerpts from the [[Hebrew Bible]] (''[[Book of Psalms|Psalms]]'' and ''[[Book of Isaiah|Isaiah]]'')<ref name=GS201/> and from the [[New Testament]] (''[[Acts of the Apostles]]'' the gospels of ''[[Gospel of Matthew|Matthew]]'', ''[[Gospel of Mark|Mark]]'' and ''[[Gospel of Luke|Luke]]'', and the epistles of ''[[Epistle to the Romans|Romans]]'', ''[[First Epistle to the Corinthians|1&nbsp;Corinthians]]'', ''[[Second Epistle to the Corinthians|2&nbsp;Corinthians]]'', ''[[Epistle to the Galatians|Galatians]]'', ''[[Epistle to the Ephesians|Ephesians]]'', ''[[First Epistle to the Thessalonians|1&nbsp;Thessalonians]]'', ''[[Second Epistle to the Thessalonians|2&nbsp;Thessalonians]]'', ''[[First Epistle to Timothy|1&nbsp;Timothy]]'', ''[[Second Epistle to Timothy|2&nbsp;Timothy]]'', ''[[Epistle to the Hebrews|Hebrews]]'', ''[[Second Epistle of Peter|2&nbsp;Peter]]'', ''[[First Epistle of John|1&nbsp;John]]'' and ''[[Epistle of James|James]]'').<ref name=GS201/><ref name=WS376>For ''Acts'', see Wolfgang Schulze, "Aspects of Udi–Iranian Language Contact", in Uwe Bläsing, Victoria Arakelova and Matthias Weinreich (eds.), ''Studies on Iran and The Caucasus: In Honour of Garnik Asatrian on the Occasion of His 60th Birthday'' (Leiden: Brill, 2015), pp. 373–401, at 376.</ref><ref>See photo of Albanian script of ''2 Corinthians'' 11:26–27 with its repetition of the phrase in [http://azer.com/aiweb/categories/magazine/ai113_folder/113_articles/113_zaza_secrets_revealed.html "I was persecuted,"] which helped unlock the key to the alphabet for Aleksidze.</ref> Text from the ''[[Gospel of John]]'', separate from the lectionary, was also found. Its text proved much more difficult to recover and on some pages it can only be identified by the [[Eusebian canons]] at the bottom of the page. This was likely a complete gospel originally,<ref name=GS201/> and it is possible that the whole Bible had at some point been translated into Caucasian Albanian.<ref name=WS376/>

The Caucasian Albanian translation of the Bible relies predominantly on [[Bible translations into Armenian|Old Armenian translations]], but it deviates from the known Armenian text in several places, suggesting that the original [[Biblical Greek|Greek]] and possibly [[Bible translations into Georgian|Georgian]] and [[Bible translations into Aramaic|Syriac]] translations were also used as source texts.<ref name=GS201/>

Apart from the Caucasian Albanian palimpsests kept at Mt. Sinai, the most famous samples of Caucasian Albanian inscriptions were found in 1949 during excavations in [[Mingachevir]] region, Azerbaijan. Among the known Caucasian Albanian words are ''zow'' (I), ''own'' (and) and ''avel-om'' (much, ordinal form).<ref name=inscrip>{{cite web|url=http://www.lrz.de/~wschulze/Cauc_alb.htm|title=Caucasian Albanian - Palimpsest and Inscriptions|publisher=Leibniz-Rechenzentrum|author=Wolfgang Schulze|year=2003|accessdate=2011-01-18|url-status=dead|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20110720004123/http://www.lrz.de/~wschulze/Cauc_alb.htm|archivedate=2011-07-20}}</ref>

